  "body": "## What changed\n\nCamCraft now has built-in, generic, parametric milling vises. They're placed like any fixture and judged by the existing fixture laws, with no new collision code.\n\n- **Geometry (`app::vise`).** Each vise is built from ten parameters plus its jaw opening, as one closed shell with outward normals. The shell is the vise's side profile (base, fixed jaw, moving jaw at the opening, and a block for the screw and handle housing), extruded across the body width. The jaws are drawn as wide as the body, which errs on the safe side. The end caps are triangulated by ear clipping, so the mesh is watertight by construction. Bad parameters are refused by name: non-finite, zero or negative values, an opening over the max, a body narrower than the jaws, or a body too short to carry both jaws plus the housing.\n- **Placement (`commands::InsertVise`).** This wraps the fixture library's `InsertFixture`, so a vise lands as an ordinary model wearing `FixtureDef`. Its parameters ride the marker (`FixtureDef::vise`), so delete/undo and re-binding to another setup keep them. Re-importing a file over a vise drops them. There are two ways to place one:\n  - fit it to the setup's stock (fixed jaw on the stock's +Y side, centred in X, floor under the stock, jaws opened to the stock's Y width);\n  - give a pose (the frame origin is the fixed jaw's clamping face, centred, at the floor).\n- **Editing (`commands::SetVise`).** It changes the jaw opening or any dimension in one undo step. The mesh is regenerated through `UpdateModel` and the revision moves. \"Open to stock\" re-opens the jaws to the stock as the vise stands, measured along the vise's own screw axis. It's refused by name if the stock reaches into the fixed jaw or is wider than the max opening.\n- **Project file.** The fixture marker gets an optional `vise` key, and the project version is not bumped. It's absent on imported fixtures. An older build ignores the key and keeps the saved facets. A vise key with invalid values is dropped and logged on load, and the fixture stays as plain steel.\n- **UI.**\n  - \"Add built-in vise…\" on the setup's Fixtures node menu and on the Fixtures page. The dialog shares the fixture library's modal: pick a size, edit any value, then Fit to the stock (the default) or type a pose.\n  - A placed vise's row shows **Jaw opening**, **Set opening** and **Open to stock**. Each one queues `SetVise`.\n- **MCP.**\n  - `add_builtin_vise`: size, setup, `fit_to_stock` or position/rotation/opening, and dimension overrides.\n  - `set_vise`: opening or `from_stock`, plus dimension overrides.\n  - Each call is one History step. No follow-up issue is needed. The `camcraft-mcp` skill's tool table is a process file, so I left it alone; it can list the two tools in the next process sync.\n- **Decisions:** `docs/decisions/788-builtin-vises.md`.\n\n## Size table\n\nThese are typical values in our own rounded, generic proportions; no vendor's model is copied. Sources are cited in the decision file. The code stores millimetres, converting inches at 25.4 mm.\n\n| Size | Jaw width | Jaw height | Jaw thick | Max opening | Length | Width | Base height | Housing L x H |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 3 in | 3 in | 1 5/16 in | 1 in | 4 3/4 in | 12 in | 4 in | 2 in | 1.5 x 0.75 in |\n| 4 in | 4 in | 1 1/4 in | 1 1/4 in | 6 in | 14 in | 5.25 in | 2.25 in | 1.75 x 1 in |\n| 5 in | 5 in | 1 1/2 in | 1 3/8 in | 7 in | 16 in | 6.25 in | 2.5 in | 2 x 1 in |\n| 6 in | 6 in | 1 3/4 in | 1 1/2 in | 9 in | 18.5 in | 7.5 in | 2.875 in | 2.25 x 1.25 in |\n| 8 in | 8 in | 2 in | 1 3/4 in | 8 1/4 in | 20 in | 9.5 in | 3.25 in | 2.5 x 1.5 in |\n| 100 mm | 100 | 32 | 25 | 110 | 300 | 125 | 55 | 35 x 20 |\n| 125 mm | 125 | 40 | 30 | 160 | 360 | 150 | 65 | 40 x 25 |\n| 150 mm | 150 | 45 | 35 | 200 | 420 | 180 | 70 | 45 x 30 |\n| 160 mm | 160 | 50 | 38 | 220 | 450 | 190 | 75 | 50 x 30 |\n| 200 mm | 200 | 62 | 45 | 260 | 550 | 240 | 85 | 60 x 35 |\n\n## How tested\n\nPinning tests:\n\n- `app builtin_vise` (6 tests):\n  - `every_table_row_builds_a_closed_shell_of_its_dimensions`: all 10 sizes at 1%, 50% and 100% opening. Checks that every directed edge has its reverse, that the outward volume is exact, the bounding dimensions, and that `FixtureMesh::build` accepts the mesh.\n  - `the_table_keeps_the_issues_starting_figures`\n  - `nonsense_parameters_are_refused_by_name`\n  - `a_placed_six_inch_vise_convicts_a_pass_across_its_jaws_and_clears_one_over_them`: the vise is judged by its `Mesh` state. A face pass at z 19.5 across the jaws strikes the fixture; a pass at 59.5, over the jaws, posts clean.\n  - `the_jaw_opening_follows_the_stock`: fit, re-open after the stock grows, and the two named refusals.\n  - `placing_and_editing_are_one_undo_each_and_survive_a_save`: undo removes the vise; a save/load round trip keeps the parameters and they stay editable.\n- `app mcp_vise`: `a_vise_is_fitted_reopened_and_undone_over_mcp`.\n- `ui` lib:\n  - `fixture_vise_tests` (4 tests): the menu and page entry, Place fitted plus undo, a refusal named in the dialog, and Set opening / Open to stock with undo.\n  - `tests::fixture_rows::a_built_in_vise_row_fits_the_default_pane` (structural).\n- Updated menu-label asserts in `fixture_library_tests` and `fixture_ux_tests`.\n\nGates:\n\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- Quick gate (`cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`): green, app 115 suites + lib (552 s), ui lib + 11 suites (69 s), 0 failed.\n- Linux batch gate: green, geometry, cl, tools, post, sim, motion, app, ui suites and clippy -D warnings, 9 steps, 0 failed (14.2 min).\n- No snapshot PNG changed. Adding the \"Add built-in vise…\" button to the Fixtures page may move a release-lane golden that shows that page; those are regenerated at the cut.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- I haven't checked the GUI by hand: opening the dialog in the running app, placing a vise, seeing it drawn in the viewport, and dragging the opening field.\n- Placing with \"Fit\" on a rotary/tilted setup; that's the #787 territory.\n\nChangelog: Built-in parametric milling vises (3-8 in, 100-200 mm) in the fixture library: placed in one undoable step, fitted to the stock, jaw opening editable, judged by the fixture mesh laws (#788)\n\nEffort: high (not escalated)\n\nfork synced at v0.16.0\n\nFixes #788\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX\n",
